# Layer Separation of Concerns

Layers: **UI → Business → DataAccess → Domain**. Dependencies point inward only. The UI may be replaced later (web, another desktop toolkit); put as much logic as possible in Business so a UI swap is mostly view/binding work, not a rewrite.

## What goes where

| Layer | Owns | Does not own |
|-------|------|--------------|
| **Domain** | Entities, enums, value rules with no I/O | EF, DTOs, UI, services |
| **DataAccess** | `DbContext`, EF configs, migrations, repositories, DB provider wiring | Business rules, DTOs for UI, ViewModels |
| **Business** | Services, DTOs, validators, mapping, orchestration, calculations, workflows | `DbContext`, EF LINQ, SQL, Avalonia/UI types |
| **UI** | Views, ViewModels, navigation, binding, user prompts, displaying errors | Direct DB access, domain persistence logic, business calculations |

## Hard rules

- **Never** reference `AppDbContext`, `DbSet<>`, EF Core, or raw SQL outside `CoperativeTelouet.DataAccess`.
- **Never** put business rules, pricing, stock movements, document numbering, or validation orchestration in ViewModels — call Business services instead.
- **Never** let UI depend on DataAccess types except thin startup wiring (DI registration, `MigrateAsync` at launch). Prefer calling Business from ViewModels.
- **Prefer** new features as Business service methods (+ DTOs/validators) first; UI only binds and invokes.
- Repositories stay dumb CRUD/query helpers; **rich behavior lives in Business services**.
- Specialized services (`ClientService`, `StockageService`, …) **must inherit `GenericService`**, never mark its members `virtual`, and add custom methods that call inherited CRUD; see `generic-service-inheritance.mdc`.
- Domain entities stay persistence-agnostic; do not decorate them with UI or EF attributes when Fluent API can own mapping.

## Examples

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — DB in UI / ViewModel
var cats = await _db.Categories.ToListAsync();

// ❌ BAD — business rule in ViewModel
if (etat == EtatBac.Plein && chambreId is null) { /* reject */ }

// ✅ GOOD — ViewModel calls Business
await _categorieService.CreateAsync(new CreateCategorieDto(Nom));

// ✅ GOOD — workflow in Business, persistence via repository
public async Task EnregistrerDepotAsync(CreateBonEntreeStockageDto dto, CancellationToken ct)
{
    // validate, compute balances, assign NumeroLot, update stock…
    await _repo.AddAsync(entity, ct);
}
```

## Dependency checklist

- UI → Business (and Domain types only if needed for generics); no EF packages in UI feature code.
- Business → DataAccess abstractions (`IRepository<>`) + Domain; no Avalonia references.
- DataAccess → Domain only for entities; no Business/UI references.

**Rule of thumb:** if the UI framework disappeared tomorrow, Business + DataAccess + Domain should still express the full product behavior.
